::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which habit is most important before making a conclusion from a Geometry diagram? {
=Look for labels, markings, and given information. #Correct. Geometry conclusions should be based on evidence shown or stated, not on appearance alone.
~Choose the conclusion that looks most likely. #This can lead to unsupported assumptions. A conclusion must be supported by diagram evidence or given information.
~Ignore labels and use the drawing size. #Labels and markings carry the mathematical information. Drawing size may not be reliable.
~Assume the diagram is always drawn to scale. #Many Geometry diagrams are not drawn to scale. Use given information and markings instead.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows points L, M, and N on one straight line. What vocabulary word describes these points? {
~Congruent #Congruent means same size and shape or equal measure. It does not describe points on the same line.
=Collinear #Correct. Points that lie on the same straight line are collinear.
~Perpendicular #Perpendicular describes lines, rays, or segments that meet at right angles, not simply points on one line.
~Supplementary #Supplementary describes two angles whose measures add to 180&deg;.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows points P, Q, and R on one straight line, with Q between P and R. Which statement is supported? {
~PQ = QR #This assumes equal lengths. The diagram would need matching tick marks, labels, or measurements to support that.
=P, Q, and R are collinear. #Correct. The points are described as lying on one straight line, so they are collinear.
~&angle;PQR is a right angle. #A right angle needs a square corner mark or a measure of 90&deg;. Collinear points form a straight path.
~P and R are endpoints of a ray. #A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction. The description does not define a ray.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Segment AB and segment CD have matching tick marks. What conclusion is supported? {
~AB is longer than CD. #Matching tick marks do not show one segment is longer. They show equal segment lengths.
~AB and CD are perpendicular. #Perpendicular describes lines or segments meeting at a right angle. Tick marks on segments show congruence.
=AB = CD #Correct. Matching tick marks on segments show congruent segments, so their lengths are equal.
~A, B, C, and D are collinear. #The tick marks do not show that all points lie on the same line.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What evidence would support the conclusion that two angles are congruent? {
~The angles are drawn near each other. #Location does not prove congruence. The diagram needs measures, markings, or given information.
~One angle is labeled and the other is not. #A single label does not show equal measures. Congruence needs evidence for both angles.
~The angles look close in size. #Appearance alone is not enough evidence in Geometry.
=The angles have matching arc marks. #Correct. Matching arc marks are diagram evidence that angles are congruent.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A square corner mark is shown at vertex K of angle JKL. What does this marking mean? {
~m&angle;JKL = 45&deg; #A square corner mark does not show 45&deg;. It marks a right angle.
=m&angle;JKL = 90&deg; #Correct. A square corner mark shows that the angle is a right angle, so its measure is 90&deg;.
~JK = KL #The square corner mark gives angle information, not side length information.
~J, K, and L are collinear #A right angle means the rays form a corner, so the points are not on one straight line in this angle.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says two segments are congruent because they look the same length. What misconception is shown? {
~The student used matching tick marks correctly. #No matching tick marks are mentioned. The student relied on appearance instead of evidence.
~The student confused points with angles. #The issue is not about points or angles. It is about assuming segment congruence from appearance.
=The student treated appearance as proof. #Correct. In Geometry, congruence must be supported by markings, measurements, given statements, or valid reasoning.
~The student used a right angle mark incorrectly. #No right angle mark is part of the situation. The misconception is unsupported segment congruence.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which response gives the clearest mathematical explanation? {
~They are equal. #This may state a conclusion, but it does not explain the evidence.
~It looks right. #This relies on appearance and does not give mathematical evidence.
=AB = CD because matching tick marks show congruent segments. #Correct. This response states the conclusion and identifies the diagram evidence.
~The answer is obvious. #This does not show reasoning, evidence, or a Geometry concept.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram labels m&angle;ABC = 60&deg;. Which conclusion is supported? {
=Angle ABC measures 60&deg;. #Correct. The angle measure is given directly by the label.
~Angle ABC is a right angle. #A right angle measures 90&deg;, not 60&deg;.
~AB = BC #An angle measure does not show that the two side lengths are equal.
~A, B, and C are collinear. #An angle measure of 60&deg; means the rays form an angle, not a straight line.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A drawing shows two lines that appear parallel, but there are no parallel markings and no statement that the lines are parallel. What is the most accurate conclusion? {
~The lines are definitely parallel. #This conclusion is not supported because appearance alone is not enough evidence.
~The lines are definitely perpendicular. #Perpendicular lines form right angles, and no right angle evidence is given.
=The lines cannot be concluded parallel from the drawing alone. #Correct. Parallelism requires markings, a given statement, or another valid reason.
~The lines must be segments. #A lack of parallel markings does not determine whether the figures are lines or segments.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which phrase best describes given information? {
~A guess based on the picture #Given information is not a guess. It is stated or shown with reliable labels and markings.
=Information stated in words or shown with labels or markings #Correct. Given information is the evidence provided in a problem or diagram.
~A conclusion that is always true for every diagram #Given information applies to the specific problem. It is not automatically true for every diagram.
~A measurement estimated by eye #Estimating by eye is not reliable given information in Geometry.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is an assumption rather than a supported conclusion? {
~Points A, B, and C are collinear when they are shown on one straight line. #This is supported by the diagram information.
~m&angle;DEF = 90&deg; when a square corner mark appears at E. #This is supported by the right angle marking.
~XY = ZW when segments XY and ZW have matching tick marks. #This is supported by congruent segment markings.
=Two unlabeled segments are congruent because they look the same length. #Correct. This is an assumption because no measurement, tick marks, or given statement support it.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What should a student do when a diagram does not provide enough evidence for a conclusion? {
~Make the most likely conclusion anyway. #This encourages unsupported reasoning. Geometry requires evidence.
~Use the longest-looking segment as the answer. #Visual length can be misleading unless supported by measurements or markings.
=State that the conclusion cannot be made without more information. #Correct. This distinguishes what is shown from what can actually be concluded.
~Ignore the diagram completely. #The diagram may still provide useful evidence; the issue is that it does not support that specific conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows ray BA and ray BC forming angle ABC. Which point is the vertex of angle ABC? {
~A #A is on one ray of the angle, but it is not the middle letter in the angle name.
=B #Correct. In angle ABC, the middle letter B names the vertex.
~C #C is on one ray of the angle, but it is not the vertex in the angle name.
~A and C #A and C help name the rays, but the vertex is the shared endpoint B.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best explains why labels matter in Geometry diagrams? {
~Labels make diagrams decorative. #Labels are not decoration. They identify points, measures, and relationships.
~Labels replace the need for reasoning. #Labels provide information, but students still need to reason from that information.
=Labels identify the objects and information used to support conclusions. #Correct. Labels help connect a conclusion to specific points, segments, angles, or measures.
~Labels always prove every possible relationship. #Labels provide evidence for some conclusions, but not every possible relationship.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A problem states that line m is parallel to line n. Which conclusion is supported? {
~Line m is perpendicular to line n. #Parallel lines and perpendicular lines are different relationships. Perpendicular lines meet at right angles.
~Line m is longer than line n. #Lines extend without fixed length, so this conclusion does not follow from parallelism.
=Line m and line n do not intersect. #Correct. Parallel lines in a plane do not intersect.
~Line m and line n form a right angle. #Lines that form a right angle are perpendicular, not parallel.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A problem states that segment RS has length 12 units. Which notation correctly represents this information? {
~RS = 90&deg; #Degrees measure angles, not segment length.
=RS = 12 #Correct. Segment length can be represented by the segment name and its numerical measure.
~m&angle;RS = 12 #Angle notation needs three letters for most named angles and uses m&angle; for angle measure, not segment length.
~R = S #This would incorrectly suggest the points are the same or equal, not that a segment has length 12.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement uses evidence from a diagram? {
~The lines are parallel because they seem straight. #Straightness does not show parallelism. Parallel evidence needs markings or a given statement.
~The angles are equal because the drawing looks balanced. #Balance in a drawing is not mathematical evidence.
~The segment is longest because it is drawn near the top. #Position in the drawing does not prove length.
=The angles are congruent because they have matching arc marks. #Correct. Matching arc marks are diagram evidence for congruent angles.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows angle XYZ with a measure of 120&deg;. Which conclusion is supported? {
~Angle XYZ is a right angle. #A right angle measures 90&deg;, not 120&deg;.
=m&angle;XYZ = 120&deg; #Correct. The measure is given directly, so this conclusion is supported.
~XY = YZ #An angle measure does not show that the side lengths are equal.
~X, Y, and Z are collinear. #A 120&deg; angle is not a straight angle, so the points are not shown as collinear by this measure.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which response correctly distinguishes what is shown from what can be concluded? {
~The segments look equal, so they must be congruent. #This does not distinguish appearance from evidence. Looking equal is not enough.
=The segments look equal, but without tick marks or measures, congruence cannot be concluded. #Correct. This separates visual appearance from a supported mathematical conclusion.
~The segments have no labels, so the diagram has no information at all. #A diagram may still show some information, but not enough for segment congruence.
~The segments are not congruent because no tick marks are shown. #No tick marks means congruence is not supported; it does not prove the segments are not congruent.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A diagram shows two rays with common endpoint T. What geometric object is formed by the two rays? {
~A segment #A segment is part of a line with two endpoints. Two rays with a common endpoint form an angle.
~A circle #A circle is the set of points the same distance from a center. Two rays do not form a circle.
=An angle #Correct. An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint.
~A midpoint #A midpoint is a point halfway between two endpoints of a segment.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student writes, "The angle is 90&deg; because it has a square corner mark." What is strongest about this explanation? {
~It uses only the size of the drawing. #The explanation does not rely on drawing size. It uses a marking.
~It guesses the angle measure from appearance. #The explanation names evidence, not a guess.
=It connects the conclusion to diagram evidence. #Correct. The student uses the square corner mark as evidence for a 90&deg; angle.
~It avoids using Geometry vocabulary. #The explanation uses Geometry ideas such as angle measure and square corner mark.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which conclusion requires more evidence? {
~A right angle measures 90&deg; when a square corner mark is shown. #This is supported by the right angle marking.
=Two drawn segments are congruent because they appear the same length. #Correct. Appearance alone does not support segment congruence.
~Points on the same straight line are collinear. #This follows from the definition of collinear points.
~Matching tick marks show equal segment lengths. #This is supported by standard diagram markings.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which sentence would be best for a short mathematical explanation? {
~Because I can see it. #This does not identify evidence or a Geometry concept.
~That is what I think. #This gives an opinion, not a mathematical explanation.
~It looks close enough. #This relies on appearance instead of evidence.
=The matching tick marks show the segments are congruent. #Correct. This is short, clear, and based on diagram evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_L01_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L01_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is the main purpose of this Geometry readiness lesson? {
~To prove all major Geometry theorems immediately #This lesson prepares reasoning habits; formal theorem proof develops later in the course.
~To memorize every formula in Geometry #This readiness lesson focuses on diagram reasoning and communication, not formula memorization.
=To build careful diagram-reading and evidence-based explanation habits #Correct. The lesson prepares students to observe, support conclusions, and avoid unsupported assumptions.
~To skip notebook explanations and rely only on answers #Notebook explanations are part of the lesson because Geometry reasoning must be visible.
}
